{"test":22,"variant":"simultaneous-2","result":{"sources":[{"title":"WHO recommendations for augmentation of labour","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241507363?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"First and Second Stage Labor Management | ACOG","url":"https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/clinical-practice-guideline/articles/2024/01/first-and-second-stage-labor-management?utm_source=openai"}],"text":"Augmentation of labour means helping labour that has already started to keep moving when the contractions are not strong or regular enough. The main methods described are oxytocin given through a drip and, sometimes, breaking the waters artificially. WHO notes that augmentation has traditionally used oxytocin and/or amniotomy, and ACOG describes these as the main methods used.  \n\nIt is different from induction, which is starting labour; augmentation is used after labour has begun. WHO also warns that it should be used carefully, because the wrong timing or amount can lead to too-strong contractions and more interventions.  \n\nIf this is being discussed for you, a useful question to ask is: “Why are you suggesting augmentation in my case, and what method would you use?”"}}
