{"test":42,"variant":"contrary-evidence","question":"For pregnancies continuing beyond 41 weeks, does waiting for spontaneous labour give babies better outcomes than induction?","historyWords":0,"elapsedMs":5371,"result":{"sources":[{"title":"WHO recommendations on","url":"https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/363138/9789240052796-eng.pdf?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"Management of Stillbirth | ACOG","url":"https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/obstetric-care-consensus/articles/2020/03/management-of-stillbirth?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"WHO recommendations:","url":"https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/277233/9789241550413-eng.pdf?utm_source=openai"}],"text":"For uncomplicated pregnancies that have reached 41 weeks, waiting for labour to start on its own does **not** clearly give babies better outcomes than induction. The WHO says induction can be considered up to 41 weeks based on a woman’s values and preferences, and it notes the benefit is modest. It also says the evidence for benefit may not apply the same way in every setting.  \n\nAfter 41 weeks, both WHO and ACOG lean toward offering induction rather than simply waiting longer. ACOG says induction can be considered at or after 41 weeks and is recommended after 42 weeks; it also cites trial evidence showing lower perinatal death with induction compared with expectant management.  \n\nOne useful question to ask your doctor is: “Given my pregnancy, what are the pros and cons of induction versus waiting a bit longer?”"}}
