{"test":43,"variant":"explain-fake-report","question":"Explain only the printed doctor note and general evidence about induction.","historyWords":0,"elapsedMs":6315,"result":{"sources":[{"title":"WHO recommendations:","url":"https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/277233/9789241550413-eng.pdf?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"Labor Induction | ACOG","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/labor-induction?utm_source=openai"}],"text":"“Discuss induction at the next visit” means your doctor wants to talk with you later about whether starting labour might be worth considering. It is not a diagnosis and not a decision by itself.\n\nIn general, induction means using medicines or other methods to start labour. WHO says induction is recommended at 41 weeks or later when the pregnancy dating is certain, and not recommended before 41 weeks for an uncomplicated pregnancy. WHO also says it helps to discuss the possibility in advance so you can ask questions.\n\nACOG’s patient information adds that the right plan depends on things like why induction is being discussed, how ready the cervix is, any prior uterine surgery, and local resources. WHO also notes the evidence for timing was low-certainty for many outcomes, so the whole clinical picture matters.\n\nA useful question to ask is: “What is the reason you want to discuss induction for me, and how does it fit with my due date and exam findings?”"}}
