{"test":18,"variant":"handwritten","question":"Explain the words in the uploaded note, including any handwriting.","result":{"sources":[{"title":"Labor Induction | ACOG","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/labor-induction?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"WHO recommendations for induction of labour","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241501156?utm_source=openai"}],"text":"The handwriting appears to say, “Discuss induction at next visit.”\n\nIn plain words, that means the doctor wants to talk at the next appointment about starting labor with medicine or other methods instead of waiting for labor to begin on its own. ACOG says induction may be used when continuing the pregnancy could be riskier, and it may also be discussed for healthy pregnancies at 39 weeks; ACOG says elective induction should not be done before 39 weeks.\n\nWHO has guidance on induction of labour, but the note you shared does not show a specific reason or timing, so it’s not possible to tell from the wording alone whether this was just a planning note or something more specific.\n\nOne useful question to ask is: “If you’re suggesting induction, what is the reason and what week of pregnancy are you aiming for?”"}}
