{"test":18,"variant":"mixed","question":"Explain the words in the uploaded note, including any handwriting.","result":{"sources":[{"title":"WHO recommendations on mechanical methods for induction of labour","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240055780?utm_source=openai"},{"title":"Medications for Pain Relief During Labor and Delivery | ACOG","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/medications-for-pain-relief-during-labor-and-delivery?utm_source=openai"}],"text":"The note is saying: “discuss induction at next visit” and “discuss pain relief options.”\n\nIn plain language, the care team wants to talk with you later about whether labor might need to be started with medical methods, and about what pain relief choices you would want during labor. “Induction” means starting labor instead of waiting for it to begin on its own.\n\nThe note does not say you definitely need induction or any one pain relief method. It only shows what was meant to be discussed. For pain relief, the discussion can include medicine options, like an epidural, and non-medicine options like massage, warm packs, water, relaxation, or support.\n\nA good question to ask at the next visit is: “Why are we discussing induction, and what pain relief choices would be available to me if I go into labor?”"}}
